MODEL 75 — ELECTROLYSIS EXPLORER

Core objective: Demonstrate how an external electrical energy source drives nonspontaneous chemical reactions in an electrolytic cell.

A. Required apparatus

Build a 3D electrolytic-cell scene containing:

- An electrolyte container.
- Two electrodes.
- A suitable DC power source.
- External wires.
- A visible electrolyte region.
- Clearly labelled anode and cathode.
- Mobile-ion representations.
- Product-formation regions where appropriate.

Show the electrical connections accurately. In an electrolytic cell, the cathode is negative and the anode is positive under the conventional DC arrangement.

B. Supported experiments

Provide a selectable experiment system with scientifically valid examples such as:

Experiment A — Molten sodium chloride

Display the appropriate ions and electrode reactions:

Cathode:

[
\mathrm{Na^+ + e^- \rightarrow Na}
]

Anode:

[
\mathrm{2Cl^- \rightarrow Cl_2 + 2e^-}
]

Overall reaction:

[
\mathrm{2NaCl(l)\rightarrow 2Na(l)+Cl_2(g)}
]

Clearly state that this example uses molten sodium chloride, not an aqueous sodium chloride solution.

Experiment B — Water electrolysis

Use a clearly specified and chemically appropriate setup. Explain the role of the electrolyte, electrode material and experimental conditions. Where the intended reaction produces hydrogen and oxygen, display correctly balanced electrode reactions and the overall reaction for the chosen conditions.

Do not apply one universal set of electrode reactions to every electrolyte.

C. Ion and electron movement

Implement separate visual representations for:

- Cations moving towards the cathode.
- Anions moving towards the anode.
- Electron transfer through the external circuit.
- Chemical changes at the electrode surfaces.
- Gas formation where appropriate to the selected reaction.

Do not show electrons travelling freely through the bulk electrolyte. Ionic movement and external-circuit electron flow must be visually and conceptually distinct.

D. Faraday's laws calculator

Include a functional calculation panel for theoretical electrolysis products where the relevant reaction and inputs are defined.

Use:

[
Q=It
]

where (Q) is charge in coulombs, (I) is current in amperes and (t) is time in seconds.

For a product requiring (z) electrons per formula unit, calculate theoretical product mass using:

[
m=\frac{MIt}{zF}
]

where:

- (m) is theoretical product mass.
- (M) is molar mass.
- (I) is current.
- (t) is duration.
- (z) is the electron-transfer number for the specified product reaction.
- (F) is the Faraday constant, approximately (96485,\mathrm{C,mol^{-1}}).

The calculator must use the correct reaction stoichiometry and appropriate units. Explain that actual yields may differ because of current efficiency, side reactions and other experimental factors.

E. Interactive controls

Implement:

1. Experiment Selector: switch between supported electrolysis setups.
2. Current Input: change the current using a validated numerical input or slider.
3. Duration Input: change the operating duration.
4. Ion Motion Animation: control and reset the visualization.
5. Electrode Inspection: select an electrode to inspect its reaction.
6. Equation Panel: show the correct reactions for the selected experiment.
7. Product Display: indicate the expected products under the specified conditions.
8. Calculation Panel: update charge and theoretical yield whenever valid inputs change.
9. Reset Experiment: restore default conditions and animation state.

All controls must work. Changing experiments must update the apparatus, electrode reactions, labels, ions, products and calculations together.

